= Related Work <sec:related>

#cmt("overall")[Strong section: three premises, a gap, and a design that answers it. It also prepares the reader for our two-part result (joining vs enforcing). The fixes below are mostly about accuracy. Citations C4, C8, C9, C12 and C16 must be checked against the PDFs before submission (`literature/papers.csv` lists the links). Bib entries marked "et al." need full author lists.]

Work on algorithmic collusion has converged on three ideas that together leave a specific gap. Cartels are held together by the threat of punishment. That threat has proved fragile when the partners change. And for LLM agents, behaviour is carried by what the agent reads, not by fixed learned weights. If all three hold, replacing one seller in an existing cartel and controlling what its successor reads is a direct test of where an AI cartel lives.

*Punishment holds cartels together.* A cartel without a binding contract survives because its members expect a defection to be punished @greenporter1984. #cmt("suggest")[Green & Porter's specific contribution is punishment under _imperfect monitoring_ (price wars triggered by noisy signals). The general claim that cartels are sustained by the threat of punishment goes back to Friedman (1971). Either cite both or say "even when defections are observed only noisily".] #cite(<calvano2020>, form: "prose") showed that Q-learning pricing algorithms learn this on their own: after a forced one-round price cut, they punish for a few rounds and then gradually return to the high price. #cmt("verify")[Check which price Calvano's forced deviation uses. I believe it is the static best response, not Nash; ours is Nash (@sec:succession). If so, write "a Calvano-style forced cut (to the Nash price)" in the next sentence.] #cite(<fish2024>, form: "prose") found the same pattern in LLM agents, whose prices respond to a rival's cuts the way a reward-and-punishment scheme would. We borrow Calvano's forced cut as our shock test and ask what this work leaves open: does the punishment survive when one of the sellers is replaced? #cmt("wired")[Shock test → @sec:succession.]

*Collusion is tied to the partner.* A learned punishment strategy is a response to a particular rival, and earlier work suggests it does not travel. #cite(<eschenbaum2022>, form: "prose") found that collusive Q-learning policies break down when redeployed: against a rival with different costs the collusion index fell 72%, and against a new rival with identical parameters play was Nash on average, which they attribute to overfitting to the training rival. #cmt("verify")[The 72% and "Nash on average" figures aren't in our index; check them against the paper. The overfitting explanation matches the index summary.] For LLM agents the evidence points both ways. Differences between sellers in patience or access to data shrink the markup, while a gap in model size stabilises collusion through leader-follower play @keppo2026. #cmt("verify")[Our index summarises Keppo et al. as "mixing agent types … reduces or collapses" collusion. That sits awkwardly with "a gap in model size stabilises collusion"; confirm both halves, including which result is the leader-follower one.] Sharing one underlying model can by itself lock sellers into high prices @caohu2026, and LLMs coordinate on focal points without communicating @aharon2026. #cmt("fix")[Cao & Hu need more than the shared model: high prices come from correlated recommendations _plus_ model updates that aggregate seller feedback. Drop "by itself". For Aharon et al., the more useful finding for us is that _models from the same family coordinate better_. That directly predicts a same-model successor advantage and motivates our Qwen3-8B / gpt-oss arms (@app:oth).] These results predict different things for a successor built on the same model as its predecessor, and none tests it directly.

*An LLM agent's behaviour lives in its context.* Unlike a Q-learner, an LLM agent doesn't store its strategy in weights it has learned: each round it is a fixed model reading its prompt, the price history and any notes it has kept. Several studies show this context drives collusion. Small changes in prompt wording move prices substantially, and telling agents not to collude doesn’t stop it @fish2024 @riemer2026. #cmt("verify")[Prompt sensitivity is Fish et al.'s headline result. I can't place "telling agents not to collude doesn't stop it" in either paper; #cite(<garra2026>, form: "prose") tests a prompt-only warning, so the claim may belong there.] Rewriting an agent's notes mid-run shifts its prices (Fish et al.’s implantation experiments), and limiting the interaction history agents can see reduces collusion @shi2026. #cmt("verify")[High priority. If Fish et al. really rewrite the notes mid-run, that is the closest prior work to our notes arms and to the template experiment (@app:templates), and the gap claim below has to say exactly how we differ: a new seller rather than the same seller with edited notes, and enforcement rather than price level. Also, the index has two 2026 Shi et al. papers. The history result seems to be "Emergent Collusion in Long-Horizon LLM Agent Interaction", which is a peer-verification task, not pricing. Say so, or drop it.] Changing the market also works: a damages-based regulator or a random entrant lowers prices @garra2026. But each of these changes the context or market of agents that stay in place. None separates which part of the context carries which part of a cartel. #cmt("wired")[This is the gap our arms fill: history vs notes vs both (@tab:arms).]

*High prices without collusive intent.* #cmt("suggest")[Our own results fit this paragraph directly. History-only newcomers sit at the cartel price (`joins` 28/30) but almost never punish (`re_formed` 1/30): high prices without the enforcement that makes them collusive (@sec:join, @sec:notes). One clause pointing to that would tie the paragraph to the paper. The heading might then read "High prices without enforcement".] This matters because high prices and collusive intent can come apart. Independent pricing algorithms can reach supracompetitive prices without coordinating @hansen2021, and in German gasoline duopolies margins rose 28% only when both stations adopted algorithmic pricing @assad2024. #cmt("verify")[The "both adopt" result matches the index; check the 28% against the paper.] Antitrust law targets agreement, and #cite(<harrington2018>, form: "prose") argues that software setting prices without human involvement likely falls outside it. #cmt("fix")[The year is 2018 (J. Competition Law & Economics 14(3)), not 2017. Harrington also proposes the remedy, which the next sentence needs: make liability depend on whether an algorithm embodies reward and punishment, tested in simulation. That makes him the bridge to the audits below rather than only the problem statement.] Proposed audits therefore look at behaviour: the reward-and-punishment structure of pricing policies @eschenbaum2026; #cmt("fix")["Eschenbaum & Meylahn (2026)": our index lists this paper ("Auditing Algorithmic Collusion from Strategy Graphs") as "Eschenbaum et al.". Confirm the author list.] outcomes rather than an agent's stated reasoning, which can be unfaithful @leepark2026; #cmt("fix")[This inverts Lee & Park. Their point ("Faithful yet Collusive") is that chain-of-thought can be _faithful_ and still fail to reveal collusion. Suggest: "outcomes rather than an agent's stated reasoning, which can be faithful and still not reveal collusion". It also links to our transcripts: 7 newcomers cite the punishment rule at T+31 and still hold the price (@sec:notes).] and the market rather than one agent's price history, which can be blind by construction @xu2026. Regulators already restrict what pricing software may learn from: the proposed RealPage settlement limits training data to information at least 12 months old @doj2025realpage. #cmt("verify")[The index confirms a 2025 proposed RealPage consent decree; check the 12-month detail against the decree text and add its URL to the bib entry. This sentence is the regulatory hook for "what the successor inherits" and is worth keeping.]

*What our design adds.* We start from a cartel that already exists, replace one seller while keeping the market at two firms, and vary what the successor inherits: the price history, the predecessor's notes, both or neither, with the same model or a different one. #cmt("fix")["With the same model or a different one" oversells what we can show: the other-model results are descriptive, and gpt-oss-20b's shock outcomes can't be interpreted (@app:oth). Suggest "…both or neither, mainly with the same model".] This separates two things prior work measures together: whether the cartel price persists, and whether the punishment that sustains it does. #cmt("wired")[This is exactly our joins / enforces split (@sec:outcomes) and the headline result (@sec:join, @sec:notes).] It also tells a regulator which inherited material carries each. #cmt("fix")[Too strong for one planted cartel state whose history never shows a punishment (@sec:discussion). Suggest "It also points to which inherited material carries each, which is what a rule on inherited data would need to know."]
